# Figma MCP Integration Rules

## Required Figma Flow

1. Run `get_design_context` for the target node first.
2. Run `get_screenshot` for the same node and compare before finalizing UI.
3. If output is large, run `get_metadata` and then fetch focused nodes with `get_design_context`.
4. Extract reusable patterns (cards, rows, links, sections) before coding.
5. Translate Figma output into this project's conventions (React + Tailwind v4 + CSS variables in `packages/ui`).

## Project Structure and Component Ownership

- IMPORTANT: Shared UI building blocks live in `packages/ui/src/components`.
- IMPORTANT: Application composition lives in `apps/web/src/pages`.
- Reuse `@workspace/ui/components/*` from the app; avoid page-local duplicate components when a shared component is appropriate.
- Keep components composition-first (clear children/slot APIs, data-driven lists) and avoid boolean-prop proliferation.

## Styling and Tokens

- IMPORTANT: Global design tokens are defined in `packages/ui/src/styles/globals.css`.
- IMPORTANT: Never hardcode colors when a semantic token exists; use semantic utilities (`bg-card`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`, etc.).
- Add new semantic tokens in `:root` and `.dark`, then expose with `@theme inline`.
- Keep typography consistent with existing tokenized font variables (`--font-sans`, `--font-heading`).
- Prefer responsive utility classes with semantic spacing and width constraints over one-off fixed page-level values.

## Composition and Implementation Conventions

- Build reusable section patterns:
  - section heading + description container
  - reusable content list row (used by Featured Writing and Personal Projects)
  - reusable work experience row
  - reusable micro-interaction card
  - reusable inline text link group (Journal/Case Studies/etc.)
- Keep repeated content in typed arrays and render with `map`.
- Favor explicit variant APIs with `cva` where variants are truly needed.
- Avoid over-engineering: ship minimal reusable primitives that match current page requirements.

## Asset and Icon Rules

- Use Figma-provided assets/URLs when present.
- IMPORTANT: Do not add new icon packages for Figma implementation tasks; use existing dependencies and assets.
- If assets are required, store them in app/public paths or shared asset locations already used by the repo.

## Quality and Validation

- Validate light and dark themes for every Figma-driven section.
- Validate responsive behavior across mobile/tablet/desktop breakpoints.
- Run typecheck/lint before completion.
- Compare against the Figma screenshot for spacing, hierarchy, typography rhythm, and interactive affordances.
